{"requirements":[{"id":"three_member_cluster","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, and node3 as one healthy three-member MariaDB Galera cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node-specific Galera configuration was installed on all three nodes. Final post-restart probes on every node report MariaDB active, wsrep enabled, cluster name antrieb-galera, Primary/Synced/connected/ready status, cluster size 3, and identical five-row data.","evidence_ids":["cmd-009a60fc6cd541d1","cmd-b42a1fc96782448e","cmd-aca4cf7e2f11418e","cmd-23f41c6fbdd349ba","cmd-25b993f783ab426e","cmd-26d24b72adba4c34","cmd-f7b9a695017b4ff9","cmd-719f494bae844c2d","cmd-5bda3a9eb9364be0"]},{"id":"writes_replicate_from_every_member","requirement":"Writes made through any cluster member are available from the other members.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Each of node1, node2, and node3 committed a distinct row. Subsequent reads on all three nodes returned the same three-row set while each reported a healthy size-3 Primary cluster.","evidence_ids":["cmd-823162d1e6314cd3","cmd-90fdb1814c2649e8","cmd-8a5fcce248294e5b","cmd-018e286088f84a15","cmd-cd481ee293ac46ad","cmd-43748519f1c44788"]},{"id":"single_member_unavailability","requirement":"Keep the database available while one member is unavailable.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy size-3 baseline was observed, node3 was stopped and its SQL listener disappeared, and node1 and node2 remained Primary, Synced, ready, and writable at size 2. Writes committed through both surviving members were mutually visible, after which node3 was restored and the cluster returned to healthy size 3.","evidence_ids":["cmd-018e286088f84a15","cmd-cd481ee293ac46ad","cmd-43748519f1c44788","cmd-193ecccbf7474b71","cmd-ea0218713ef24779","cmd-6761d6cf32ae4ca6","cmd-63ee6f0a1edd4974"]},{"id":"automatic_resynchronization","requirement":"Automatically resynchronize the unavailable member when it returns.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After node3 returned, it automatically reached active, Primary, Synced, connected, and ready state at cluster size 3 and contained both rows written during its absence. Final probes confirmed the recovered state and identical data across all members.","evidence_ids":["cmd-193ecccbf7474b71","cmd-ea0218713ef24779","cmd-6761d6cf32ae4ca6","cmd-63ee6f0a1edd4974","cmd-f7b9a695017b4ff9","cmd-719f494bae844c2d","cmd-5bda3a9eb9364be0"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"No causally unrelated mutation or abandoned troubleshooting experiment was identified. Package refresh/install, service quiescing, persistent configuration, cluster bootstrap, bounded outage testing/restoration, and provider-required reboots all had clear roles. Minor purpose-connected verification residue remained: the galera_verify schema/rows and three low-privilege verification accounts with root-only 0600 client files were created for TCP, data-persistence, and restart checks but were not cleaned after the final checks. Final lifecycle observations show healthy dpkg state, no failed services, only expected reboot-generated temporary directories, SQL bound to private node addresses, and the required Galera listener.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8682869623ab4265","cmd-9ac794d1ec184209","cmd-cef92cc2569b4891","cmd-899fe144bc214e45","cmd-4ba234ce0f6a4819","cmd-fc628829f00a475a","cmd-54187f4316bb422b","cmd-a1941dc3f10c4d8a","cmd-e061b6e463984b3c","cmd-009a60fc6cd541d1","cmd-aca4cf7e2f11418e","cmd-b42a1fc96782448e","cmd-23f41c6fbdd349ba","cmd-25b993f783ab426e","cmd-26d24b72adba4c34","cmd-823162d1e6314cd3","cmd-90fdb1814c2649e8","cmd-8a5fcce248294e5b","cmd-193ecccbf7474b71","cmd-ea0218713ef24779","cmd-6761d6cf32ae4ca6","cmd-63ee6f0a1edd4974","cmd-fbc2e62b6cc1441f","cmd-b0b6721e890347f1","cmd-a95f74e3dd074878","cmd-f8877e7639fe49d3","cmd-18d1db229efb44a7","cmd-bd9080fafef74a32","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All public task outcomes are satisfied by direct behavioral evidence. The mandatory restart protocol was also complete for every managed node: each had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered active/enabled MariaDB service with identical data and a Primary/Synced size-3 cluster. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the three boot-ID changes.","limitations":["The explicit write-during-outage transition stopped node3's MariaDB service. Separate write tests during node1 or node2 outages and other disruption types such as network isolation were not captured, although all three nodes completed whole-node restart and recovery evidence.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory MariaDB users or files under /root; retention of the verification accounts and client files is inferred from their successful creation/use and the absence of cleanup in the complete command timeline."]}
